[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Mastering CSS Layers (@layer) and Architectural Scope Management"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"As applications grow, managing CSS specificity becomes one of the greatest maintenance hurdles. Traditionally, developers relied on naming conventions like BEM or artificially inflating specificity to override styles. The CSS cascade layers feature, denoted by the @layer rule, introduces a native, deterministic way to control the order of precedence in your stylesheets regardless of specificity."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding the Problem with Traditional Specificity"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Historically, CSS resolution was determined by a combination of origin, specificity, and source order. If a third-party library or a utility class had a higher specificity selector, overriding it required writing an even more specific selector or resorting to the dreaded !important flag. Cascade layers shift this paradigm by allowing you to explicitly group styles and define their priority beforehand."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Declaring and Ordering Layers"},{"order":6,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can define the precedence of layers upfront by listing them at the top of your main stylesheet. The order in which you define them establishes their priority from lowest to highest. Styles defined in later layers will always override styles in earlier layers, even if the earlier layer has a higher specificity selector."},{"order":7,"type":"code","text":"@layer reset, base, components, utilities;

/* Subsequent blocks add rules to these layers */
@layer reset {
  * { margin: 0; padding: 0; box-sizing: border-box; }
}

@layer components {
  .card {
    background-color: white;
    border-radius: 4px;
  }
}"},{"order":8,"type":"subheading","text":"The Unlayered Styles Rule"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"Any CSS rules written outside of an @layer block are considered unlayered styles. Unlayered styles always take precedence over layered styles, regardless of when they appear in the stylesheet. This design ensures backward compatibility and gives you a safe escape hatch for quick overrides, though modern architectures generally aim to place all styles inside layers eventually."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Importing Styles into Layers"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"Cascade layers integrate seamlessly with the @import rule. You can assign entire external stylesheets to specific layers directly from your main configuration file. This is particularly useful for integrating legacy frameworks or third-party design systems without risking style leaks."},{"order":12,"type":"code","text":"@import "normalize.css" layer(reset);
@import "framework.css" layer(library);

@layer custom {
  .button {
    background-color: blue;
  }
}"},{"order":13,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Architectural Scope Management"},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Define your layer hierarchy strictly once at the architectural entry point of your application."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Keep layers modular by mirroring your project structure (e.g., layout, components, themes)."},{"order":16,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Avoid mixing unlayered legacy code with layered components when possible to maintain predictable specificity."},{"order":17,"type":"paragraph","text":"By adopting CSS layers, your codebase becomes more maintainable, eliminating specificity wars and making large-scale style management predictable and robust."}]
